From a71817459fa446b84bc782ca232ecadc32df399d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 20:05:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0082 -- exhaustive closes-by-construction
 tick partition

Three full instrumentation rounds (0079/0080/0081) left ~62% of the
reconciled steady-state budget unaccounted for, despite naming and
measuring 7 candidate phases. Per operator instruction, stopped
guessing individual functions and checked whether real profiling is
viable on this toolchain first.

gprof confirmed non-viable, empirically, not assumed: DJGPP's libc.a
genuinely has the full profiling runtime (_mcount, __monstartup,
gcrt0.o all present and correctly linked -- confirmed by inspecting
the toolchain directly), but a minimal, isolated DJGPP test program
built with -pg and run under DOSBox-X for a real 23-second workload
produced a GMON.OUT that gprof could parse as a valid file yet recorded
ZERO time samples and ZERO call-graph arcs for the test's own hot
functions (only a single incidental libc sample). Not "sparse
sampling" -- a structurally broken histogram/arc mechanism for this
target. Not chased further (the goal was a working profiler, not a
profiler bug hunt); cleaned up and moved to the fallback.

Falls back to an EXHAUSTIVE, closes-BY-CONSTRUCTION manual partition
instead of more named guesses:

- game_tick_outer: GameTick() bracketed as a whole -- confirmed to be
  the true single top-level per-outer-iteration entry point (calls
  dos_run_tick_fixed() when fixed-timestep is active, else
  UpdateGameOnce() directly, nothing else). If this phase's own sum
  does not reach ~100% of the steady-state budget, that is direct
  proof something OUTSIDE the named call graph entirely is
  responsible, not another function to name.
- update_game_once_total: UpdateGameOnce() bracketed as a whole, a
  strict superset of every other per-tick phase -- the accounting
  check for what's still unnamed inside one logic tick.
- Three real content-scaling candidates found by reading
  main/update.cpp in full for the first time (a whole file dedicated
  to per-tick object/character updates, reached via
  GameUpdateGameState() -> update_game_objects(), never previously
  read): game_objects_update (loops over croom->numobj and
  game.numcharacters via update_cycling_views/
  update_character_move_and_anim), objects_scale (a SEPARATE pass
  looping over both again, via UpdateDrawableObjectStates()), and
  gui_poll (loops over every GUI panel via update_cursor_over_gui()).
  All three confirmed non-recursive (no RunScriptFunction/
  processallevents call in ac/characterinfo_engine.cpp,
  ac/roomobject.cpp, or Common/gui/guimain.cpp).
- Every remaining call site directly inside UpdateGameOnce(), grouped
  where adjacent: ground_interactions, input_handling, room_check,
  gui_context_cursor, persistent_anims, end_of_tick. Deliberately NOT
  bracketed: GameUpdateProcessEvents() (a thin wrapper around
  processallevents(), whose own script-callback execution script_vm
  already counts -- bracketing it separately would double-count).

17 total tracked phases now (up from 6). quit.cpp's own emission
refactored to a small helper (dos_port_tick_emit()) instead of a
repeated fprintf per phase.

